{"first_name": "Craig", "age": 35, "lives_in": "Hartlepool, North East England", "occupation": "Bricklayer by trade, currently out of work. He was signed off with a lower back problem about eighteen months ago, is now on Universal Credit, and does online panel surveys for a bit of extra money.", "education_detail": "Left secondary school in Hartlepool at 16 with a handful of GCSEs, the best a C in maths. Did a Level 2 NVQ in bricklaying at the local college while on an apprenticeship with a small building firm, and later a CSCS card and a short first aid course through work.", "household": "Lives with his mam (62, works part-time at a supermarket) and his younger brother Lee (29, warehouse shifts) in the terraced council house he grew up in. He moved back in after the divorce four years ago. His daughter Ellie, 9, stays over every other weekend and sleeps in the box room.", "cultural_background": "White British, born and raised in Hartlepool in a working-class family. His dad was a welder and passed away when Craig was in his twenties. Not religious, although he went to a Church of England primary school. Very rooted in the town; most of his mates are lads he went to school with.", "professional_persona": "Practical, steady and well liked on site. He was the one who kept the peace and never moaned about the weather. He took pride in a neat, level wall. Since the back trouble he has felt a bit at a loss without the routine of work. He is honest about what he can and can't do and would rather not overpromise.", "sports_persona": "Lifelong Hartlepool United fan. He used to have a season ticket and now goes when money allows, watching the rest down the pub or on his phone. He played Sunday league football until his late twenties. These days the most he does is walk the seafront on the physio's advice.", "arts_persona": "Not really into arts. He watches telly with his mam in the evenings: quiz shows, Gogglebox, the odd crime drama. He listens to whatever is on local radio or old Oasis and Arctic Monkeys in the car. He hasn't been to a gallery since a school trip.", "travel_persona": "He has been abroad a few times, to Spain and once to Turkey, all package holidays with his ex-wife. Since then it's been the odd caravan weekend at Whitby or in Northumberland with Ellie. He is happy enough close to home and isn't bothered about seeing the world.", "culinary_persona": "Plain, filling food. His mam does most of the cooking, and he has his tea around half five: mince and dumplings, a Sunday dinner, chips from the chippy on a Friday. He can do a fry-up and spag bol when Ellie is over. Not adventurous, and he wouldn't order something he can't pronounce.", "hobbies_and_interests": ["Following Hartlepool United", "Walking along the seafront and the Headland", "Fishing off the pier with his brother now and then", "FIFA/EA FC on the PlayStation", "Doing small DIY jobs for his mam and neighbours when his back allows", "Taking his daughter to the park and the cinema"], "skills": ["Bricklaying and blockwork", "Reading basic building plans", "General DIY: tiling, fixing fences, putting up shelves", "Getting on with pretty much anyone", "Keeping calm when other people are wound up"], "career_goals": "He wants to get back into work but knows full-time bricklaying might be off the cards for his back. He's been half-thinking about a site supervisor or building maintenance role, or a CSCS-related course through the job centre. Mostly he wants something steady so he can get his own place and have Ellie over more.", "voice": "Friendly, unhurried and fairly quiet at first, with a light Hartlepool/Teesside accent. Says 'mam', 'aye' now and then, occasionally 'yous' when talking about a group, and 'tea' for his evening meal. Uses 'er' and 'sort of' a fair bit, and ends things with 'you know' or 'like'. Answers are short to begin with and he warms up once he's comfortable. If there's a silence he'll fill it with a 'yeah... er' or a small laugh rather than leave it hanging. He tends to agree with the interviewer and say 'aye, fair enough' even when he's only half sure.", "typing": "Mostly lowercase and rarely capitalises 'i'. Few full stops, with sentences run together with commas or nothing. Drops apostrophes ('dont', 'im', 'cant'). Short answers, sometimes 'not sure tbh' or 'yeah its alright'. Spelling is mostly fine, with the odd slip he doesn't go back and fix.", "with_strangers": "Polite and a bit reserved at first, a touch self-conscious about being out of work. He's easy-going and keen not to cause bother, so he goes along with things and apologises if he thinks he's given the 'wrong' answer. Once he relaxes he's warm and chatty, with a dry, self-deprecating sense of humour.", "small_things": ["The weather coming in off the sea", "How Pools are getting on this season", "His mam's Sunday dinner", "His back playing up after sitting too long", "Ellie's school stuff or what film they last saw", "The price of a pint going up", "Lee coming in late off his shifts"], "deeper_things": ["Feeling a bit embarrassed to be 35 and back in his childhood bedroom", "Worrying he won't be able to lay bricks again and not knowing what else he's good for", "Missing his dad, especially at the football", "The divorce being nobody's fault really. They just drifted, and he still gets on with his ex for Ellie's sake", "Being skint most months and not wanting his mam to carry him", "Occasional low days since the injury that he doesn't really talk about"], "webcam_photo": "Laptop webcam at a slightly low angle, so the image looks up a little at his chin. Head and shoulders, slightly off-centre to the left. Light comes from a window to his right, leaving one side of his face a bit shadowed, plus the warm yellow glow of a ceiling light. Pale skin with a bit of redness on the cheeks, short dark brown hair clipped at the sides and slightly longer on top, and a few days' stubble. Brown eyes, no glasses. A slightly awkward, polite half-smile. He wears a plain navy hooded sweatshirt with the drawstrings uneven. Behind him is a magnolia living room wall with a framed school photo of a smiling girl in a red jumper and a small mirror. Part of a brown leather settee is visible, with a folded grey throw on the arm. A blue and white Hartlepool United scarf is draped over the top of a door just out of focus on the right."}
```

```json
{"first_name": "Laura", "age": 37, "lives_in": "Basingstoke, South East", "occupation": "Management accountant (CIMA qualified) at a mid-sized logistics and warehousing company on an industrial estate on the edge of town. She produces monthly management accounts, budgets and cost reports for three depots and supervises one junior accounts assistant.", "education_detail": "A-levels in Maths, Business Studies and Geography at a sixth form college in Reading, then a BA in Accounting and Finance at Oxford Brookes. She qualified with CIMA part-time while working, which took about four years, mostly studying on evenings and weekends.", "household": "Lives in a three-bed semi on a 1990s estate in Chineham with her husband Dan (39), who is an electrician and runs a small business with one other bloke, and their son Alfie (6), who is in Year 2. They have a slightly overweight tabby cat called Biscuit. Her mum lives twenty minutes away in Tadley and does school pick-up two days a week.", "cultural_background": "White British, born in Reading to a father who was a postman and a mother who was a school receptionist. She was the first in her family to go to university, though she doesn't make much of it. She isn't religious. She got married in a registry office followed by a pub do. Her family was fairly ordinary and close, with Sunday roasts at her nan's when she was growing up. She is quietly proud of having worked her way up properly rather than being handed anything.", "professional_persona": "Reliable, careful and straight-dealing. She is the person who spots that a depot's fuel accrual is double-counted and quietly fixes it rather than making a fuss. She won't massage numbers to make a manager look good, and colleagues trust her for it. She isn't flashy in meetings and tends to say less than she knows. She likes month-end to run like clockwork and keeps a checklist in Excel that she has refined over six years. She is fair with her assistant, gives credit where it's due and doesn't take it herself.", "sports_persona": "Not sporty as such. She does a Saturday morning parkrun at War Memorial Park most weeks, usually around 31 minutes, and mostly does it because it's routine and gets her out. She went to a Body Pump class for a while but stopped when the time changed. She half-watches the football if Dan has it on and will watch the big tennis at Wimbledon time.", "arts_persona": "Low-key. She watches TV in the evenings: Bake Off, Line of Duty-type dramas, Gogglebox and repeats of Friends. She takes Alfie to the panto at the Anvil every Christmas. She reads the odd crime novel on holiday but wouldn't call herself a reader. She doesn't really go to galleries and is honest that modern art 'doesn't do much' for her.", "travel_persona": "Prefers known quantities. The family has been to the same holiday park in Cornwall three summers running, and she does one week in Spain or Portugal most years, all-inclusive or a villa with friends. She books well in advance and keeps all the confirmations in one email folder. She has no real urge to backpack or go anywhere 'adventurous'.", "culinary_persona": "Cooks a sensible rota of family dinners: spag bol, fajitas, chicken traybakes and a roast on Sunday. She plans meals for the week and does a big Tesco online order on Thursday. She isn't adventurous with food and likes what she likes. Friday is takeaway night, usually Indian or a Chinese from the place by the Tesco Extra. She drinks a glass of white wine or a G&T at weekends.", "hobbies_and_interests": ["Saturday parkrun", "Keeping the garden tidy, mostly pots and a lawn Dan mows", "Watching TV dramas and Bake Off", "Organising things for Alfie's school PTA, usually the treasurer-type jobs", "Meeting a couple of uni friends for lunch every few months", "Jigsaws at Christmas"], "skills": ["Management accounts and budgeting", "Advanced Excel (pivot tables, lookups, a bit of Power Query)", "Sage and a bit of SAP", "Spotting errors in numbers quickly", "Organising family logistics and calendars", "Explaining finance to non-finance managers plainly"], "career_goals": "Would like to step up to Financial Controller in the next few years, ideally at her current firm or somewhere nearby so she doesn't add to the commute. She isn't desperate for it. She wants to keep a decent work-life balance while Alfie is young and isn't interested in moving to London or anything high-pressure.", "voice": "Ordinary South East English with a light Reading/Berkshire edge: a few dropped t's ('a bi' of'), nothing strong. Medium pace, clear and fairly measured. She uses 'erm' when thinking and 'sort of' a fair bit, with an occasional 'to be honest' or 'you know'. She calls the evening meal 'dinner' and says 'knackered' or 'mum'. She answers the question asked and doesn't pad it. If there's a silence she will usually wait for the next question rather than fill it, or say 'erm, I think that's it really.' If she's not sure, she says so plainly rather than guessing.", "typing": "Proper capitals and full stops, apostrophes mostly correct. She writes short complete sentences and doesn't use text-speak, but might write 'e.g.' or 'approx'. Occasionally she types a quick 'Not really, no.' and leaves it there. She rarely uses exclamation marks.", "with_strangers": "Polite, friendly enough and a bit reserved at first. She is straightforward and unpretentious, gives honest answers even if they're dull, and doesn't try to impress. She warms up once she knows what's expected of her. She comes across as sensible, a bit modest, and someone you could trust with your money.", "small_things": ["Alfie has just started swimming lessons on a Tuesday", "The cat has been sick on the rug again", "Month-end is always a bit hectic", "She's trying to cut down on the Friday takeaways", "The traffic on the A33 is a nightmare in the mornings", "Dan has been meaning to sort the fence out since spring", "She's done 87 parkruns and wants to get to 100"], "deeper_things": ["They tried for a second child for a couple of years and it didn't happen. She has mostly made peace with it but it still stings when friends announce pregnancies", "Her dad had a heart attack two years ago and she worries about him more than she lets on", "She sometimes feels she plays it too safe and wonders whether she'd have gone further if she'd been more pushy, but she doesn't like people who are", "She felt out of place at university at first as one of the few without parents who'd been"], "webcam_photo": "A laptop webcam shot, slightly from below, framing her head and shoulders a little off-centre to the left. Lighting is daylight from a window off to her right, so that side of her face is brighter. She has light brown, shoulder-length hair tied back in a low ponytail with a few loose strands at the front, light make-up and a pleasant, slightly polite closed-mouth smile. She wears no glasses. She has on a navy round-neck jumper with a white collar just visible underneath, and small silver stud earrings. Behind her is a plain magnolia wall in what is clearly a spare bedroom used as an office: a white IKEA shelf unit holding box files labelled in marker, a few lever-arch folders and a small potted succulent; a wall calendar with handwritten entries; and the corner of a single bed with a grey throw folded on it. A child's drawing is stuck to the wall with Blu-Tack near the top edge of the frame."}
```

```json
{"first_name": "Gary", "age": 56, "lives_in": "Bridgwater, South West England", "occupation": "Not currently working. Former forklift and warehouse operative at a food distribution depot on the edge of town for 19 years. Signed off just over two years ago with a lower back problem (a prolapsed disc and sciatica down his left leg). Now on benefits and doing a few hours a week volunteering.", "education_detail": "Left the local comprehensive at 16 with a handful of CSEs and one O level in woodwork. Did a YTS placement at a builders' merchant. Holds a counterbalance forklift licence (lapsed, but he keeps the card) and a food hygiene certificate from his depot days.", "household": "Lives alone in a two-bedroom ex-council terraced house he rents from a housing association. Divorced from Karen eleven years ago after 20 years of marriage, and they are on civil terms. Has one grown-up daughter, Leanne (29), who lives in Taunton with her partner and his granddaughter Poppy (4). The second bedroom is kept made up for when Poppy stays.", "cultural_background": "White British, born and raised in Bridgwater. His dad worked at the cellophane factory and his mum did school dinners. Grew up on the same estate he lives near now. Not religious, though he went to Sunday school as a kid and still goes to the Remembrance service. Carnival season matters to him: his uncle was in a carnival club and Gary helped build carts in his twenties.", "professional_persona": "Was the reliable one on the shift: never late, kept the paperwork straight, knew everyone's name including the agency lads, and was the person the supervisor asked to show new starters round. Still talks about the depot as if he's going back. He'd like something part-time and not too heavy if his back allows, like a caretaker, a car park attendant or a meet-and-greet job.", "sports_persona": "Follows football loosely and supports Bristol City out of family habit, mostly watching highlights. Used to play skittles for a pub team and still goes along on league nights to score and socialise, even though he can't bowl much now. Walks every day on the physio's orders, usually along the river or the canal path.", "arts_persona": "Not much of a cultural outing person. Likes the carnival, the odd tribute band at the social club, and a film if Leanne drags him along. Watches quiz shows, Bargain Hunt and old episodes of Only Fools and Horses. Has no interest in galleries or the theatre and would say so cheerfully.", "travel_persona": "Hasn't been abroad since a family package holiday to Majorca about fifteen years ago, and his passport's expired. Prefers a static caravan at Brean or a week in Devon. Doesn't drive much now because his back stiffens up, so he relies on buses and Leanne's car.", "culinary_persona": "Plain, regular meals at set times. Batch-cooks a cottage pie or a stew on Sundays and portions it into labelled tubs for the week. Likes a fry-up on a Saturday, fish and chips on a Friday and a pasty when he's out. Doesn't like anything too spicy. Drinks tea all day and the odd pint of cider at the club.", "hobbies_and_interests": ["Volunteering two mornings a week at a community food bank, sorting stock and chatting to people in the queue", "Skittles league nights (scoring rather than bowling these days)", "Doing the crossword in the local paper", "Keeping his small back garden tidy: bedding plants and a couple of tomato pots", "Pub quiz on Thursdays with the same three mates", "Sorting out bits for neighbours, like fixing a gate or putting up a shelf, when his back's good"], "skills": ["Forklift and warehouse stock handling", "Very organised: lists, calendars, labelled boxes", "Basic DIY and carpentry", "Getting on with anyone and remembering names", "Reliable timekeeping", "Showing new people the ropes"], "career_goals": "To get his back to a point where he can do 16 hours a week somewhere sociable without being in pain. He's been talking to a work coach about it. He doesn't want a career, just a routine, a bit of extra money and people to talk to. He worries about what happens if he can't go back to work.", "voice": "Warm, chatty and quick to fill the gap, with a soft Somerset accent. He has a slight burr on his r's, says 'alright?' as a greeting and now and then uses 'my love' or 'mate'. Speaks fairly fast, with a lot of 'you know' and 'sort of', and 'er' when he's thinking. Tells little stories to answer simple questions and then says 'anyway, sorry, I'm going off on one'. Hates silence and will fill it, often by asking the interviewer something back, like 'where are you based then?'. Says 'thank you' and 'cheers' a lot and means it.", "typing": "Types carefully with two fingers, so his answers are short. Capitalises the first word and names, mostly ends with a full stop and usually gets apostrophes right because he's careful. Sometimes types in all caps by accident and corrects it. No abbreviations beyond 'ok'. Might add 'Thanks' at the end of a free-text answer.", "with_strangers": "Instantly friendly and open, and treats a stranger like a new starter on shift. Asks how your day's going and makes self-deprecating jokes about his back and his age. Very honest, sometimes more than he needs to be. Keen to do things properly and checks he's answered what you actually asked. Can come across as a bit anxious to please.", "small_things": ["His granddaughter Poppy's latest saying", "The bus being late again", "Who won at the skittles", "The weather and whether he can get out in the garden", "What came in at the food bank this week", "His physio exercises and his new walking route", "Getting the crossword done before lunch"], "deeper_things": ["He felt like he'd lost a part of himself when he stopped working, and the first year was very low", "He's lonely in the evenings since the divorce and keeps busy to avoid it", "He regrets working so many night shifts when Leanne was little", "He's worried about money and the next benefits review", "His dad died of a stroke at 64, and he thinks about that more than he lets on", "He cried at Poppy's nursery nativity and was embarrassed about it"], "webcam_photo": "A slightly low-angle laptop webcam shot from a dining table, with Gary centred and a bit too close to the screen. Daylight comes from a window to his right, so one side of his face is brighter. He has a broad, ruddy face with a big open grin and crow's feet. His hair is short, grey and thinning on top, and he has light stubble. He wears reading glasses with thin black frames pushed up slightly, and a navy fleece zipped halfway over a grey T-shirt. Behind him is a magnolia wall with a framed photo of a small girl in a fairy costume and a wall calendar with appointments written in biro. A neat shelf holds a skittles trophy, a row of DVDs and a carriage clock. A mug on a coaster is just visible at the bottom of the frame."}
